{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal adds a bilattice component with a monotone operator to distinguish two kinds of undecidedness, but the self-criticism concedes that proving the operator monotone in both lattice orders is nontrivial. This concession is fatal to the problem's explicit guarantee of polynomial cost: a bilattice-based construction whose monotonicity (and hence the polynomial fixed-point bound) does not already come for free is precisely the kind of mechanism that risks losing the cost guarantee, and the target does not give any argument, reduction, or construction showing the new operator preserves the polynomial fixed-point complexity of the original grounded extension. Thus the strongest substance-level failure is that the proposal as stated does not substantiate the polynomial-cost preservation it is required to guarantee.","target":"5d10d63003ca79a672fb9f6ba5fc3b39b0ef5f6f7cf4cfd68f381afd92785e71"}]}